--TEST--
`Core\Serialize::decode`'s operand is a taint sink whatever route the bytes arrived by — a local, a concatenation, a member's return type, an array element, a repeated cast, taint composed with `secret`, or `encode` over a value that carries outside text — while untainted bytes decode — `rule:security/sink-predicate`, `rule:classes/serialize-is-a-closed-format`, `rule:security/taint-propagation`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// `decode` names classes and rebuilds their declared properties, so an
// attacker-chosen byte string picks which class the program is handed. `rule:security/sink-predicate`
// therefore declares the parameter `bytes` under `Qual::Sink`, which makes
// every refusal below an ordinary assignability failure rather than a rule of
// the member's own — and that is the point being pinned: there is no
// sink-named launderer in front of this sink, so the only way past is the
// written `Core\Taint::assertTrustedBytes`, which none of the routes uses.
//
// The agreement shape (`docs/agent/conventions.md`): seven routes, one question,
// so a taint propagation that grew a hole in one of them fails here while the
// other six still look right.
class Source {
    public static function payload(): tainted bytes {
        return "t" as bytes;
    }
}

tainted string $t = "t";
tainted bytes $tb = "t" as bytes;
secret tainted bytes $stb = "t" as bytes;
array<tainted bytes> $bag = [$tb];

// The accepted side first, so the refusals below are a bound with both sides
// named rather than a member that refuses everything.
bytes $clean = "n" as bytes;
mixed $ok = Core\Serialize::decode($clean);
echo $ok;

// Directly, which is the route the neighbouring `secret` case already has.
mixed $a = Core\Serialize::decode($tb);
echo $a;

// Composed: taint travels through concatenation on its own axis, so the
// argument's inferred type carries it even though nothing here is declared.
mixed $b = Core\Serialize::decode(("x" . $t) as bytes);
echo $b;

// Declared one frame away. The call site reads the member's return type, which
// is where a sink that only looked at variables would miss it.
mixed $c = Core\Serialize::decode(Source::payload());
echo $c;

// Out of a container, where the qualifier is on the element type.
mixed $d = Core\Serialize::decode($bag[0]);
echo $d;

// Composed with `secret`. Two axes, and neither launders the other: this one
// is refused for its taint, and the message says so by naming both.
mixed $e = Core\Serialize::decode($stb);
echo $e;

// A cast is not a launderer, and repeating it is not either — `as bytes` is a
// conversion of the type, not of the provenance. The way in is a checked
// conversion through a parser that produces untainted output.
mixed $f = Core\Serialize::decode($t as bytes as bytes);
echo $f;

// Round-tripped through `encode`: a `mixed` value may carry outside text, so
// the encoder's result is `tainted bytes` and the sink refuses it.
mixed $m = 1;
mixed $g = Core\Serialize::decode(Core\Serialize::encode($m));
echo $g;
--EXPECT--

